TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction - 'Thus, only one satellite is required'"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction - customers implied (marine/conservation scientists); operator never explicitly identified"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design - Launch (Vega E from Kourou)"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design - Orbit - elements given but inclination (87.79°) and semi-major axis (7171 km) values are erroneous"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design - Orbit - 'the only orbital manoeuvre occurs at the EOL segment'"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications - Mt Pleasant Observatory with STK access summary"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL - five-year life, drag-sail disposal"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Orbit/Ground Control sections - STK 11 used with plots and reports"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Screenshots included in body, but no appendix or statement that STK files are provided"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Structure is logical, but Wikipedia is cited (disallowed) and figures/tables are unnumbered"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit","issue":"Inclination 87.79° is prograde and not sun-synchronous at 600 km (~97.8° required); contradicts the student's own inclination-vs-altitude plot and the 'retrograde' description."},{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit (COE table)","issue":"Semi-major axis 7171 km is wrong: 6371 + 600 = 6971 km."},{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","issue":"Kourou's latitude stated as 45.05°N; actual is ~5.2°N."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claims 'almost twenty minutes' mean contact, but the STK table shows mean duration 580.786 s (~9.7 min)."},{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit (COE table)","issue":"RAAN 27.33° said to indicate launch facility location, inconsistent with Kourou (~52°W)."},{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit","issue":"Text states low resolution from high altitude 'is also desirable' - contradicts the resolution requirement."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Reference [6] is Wikipedia, explicitly not an acceptable source."}],"overall":"A well-structured, plausible mission concept (reef-monitoring SSO satellite) with genuine STK evidence covering nearly all requirements. However, multiple numerical errors (inclination, semi-major axis, site latitude, contact duration), Wikipedia sourcing, and unnumbered figures keep it at Adequate rather than Accomplished.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Mission, rationale, and single-spacecraft choice are well covered with references. However, users are only implied: customers (marine/conservation scientists) are hinted at, but the operator is never explicitly identified, so not all requirements are fully addressed.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost"},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"A near-real-time relay to monitoring stations on the ground would allow marine and conservation scientists"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"All major requirements addressed with STK evidence (3D views, inclination plot, access report, ground tracks). However, several technical errors: 87.79° inclination contradicts the student's own plot (~97.8°) and the 'retrograde' claim; a=7171 km should be 6971 km; Kourou latitude 45.05°N is wrong (~5.2°N); 'twenty minutes' contradicts the 580.8 s mean. Content adequate but not fully accurate.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit","quote":"an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 87.7924^{\\circ} $"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"a 'drag sail' – a drag-enhancing deorbit device that deploys at the EOL"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions follow from the report's evidence and acknowledge a real risk (Vega E availability) with a fallback. However, the conclusion is brief and generic in places, reiterating design choices rather than synthesising deeper insight.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear structure with headings, TOC and executive summary; references cited in text. But figures/tables lack numbers, Wikipedia is used (explicitly disallowed), and there are grammar/typo issues ('purposed', 'which is also desirable', broken drag-sail sentence). Formatting inconsistent in places.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Mission Design - Orbit","quote":"STK 3D view of orbit: 12 hours apart"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"for the purposed of this satellite"}]}]}
